@startuml PasstheKeys
title PasstheKeys - frontend nonce-hijack across three separate systems

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName sans-serif
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam ParticipantPadding 14
skinparam BoxPadding 12

actor "Victim" as V
box "The website (Acme Workspace)" #EEF1FF
  participant "Front-end\nvictim's tab (/login)" as VP
  participant "Back-end\nthe app + its account data" as WB
end box
participant "Pocket ID\npasskey auth only" as PID
box "Attacker's site (separate origin)" #FDECEC
  participant "Attacker's tab" as AP
  participant "Back-end\ndead-drop + proxy" as AD
end box
actor "Attacker" as A

note across
  Three separate systems. Pocket ID only proves who you are (passkey auth). The website is
  NOT Pocket ID: its own back-end holds the account data and is what the page presents.
  The attacker's site is a different origin. The attacker never signs anything.
end note

== Attacker starts a passkey login, but will not finish it ==
A -> AP : open the attacker site
AP -> PID : start a passkey login
PID --> AP : nonce **Ca** (challenge)
AP -> AD : publish **Ca** in the dead-drop

== Victim tries to log into the website ==
V -> VP : click "Sign in"
VP -> AD : fetch the nonce (cross-origin) -> **Ca**
VP -> V : passkey prompt - sign **Ca**
V --> VP : assertion over **Ca** (bound to the WEBSITE's origin)
VP -> AD : hand the assertion back
VP -> V : "that did not work" - the victim never finished a login, so gets no session

== Attacker finishes the login, then reads the victim's data ==
AP -> AD : take the assertion
AP -> PID : finish the passkey login with it
PID --> AP : a session for the victim  (Pocket ID did AUTH only)
AP -> WB : "give me the account" - with the victim's session
WB --> AP : the victim's account data  (the back-end is where the data lives)
AP -> A : present it: "Welcome back, <victim>"

note across #FFF3E0
  Pocket ID says who; the website's back-end holds what. By hijacking the passkey login the
  attacker gets the victim's session and pulls the victim's data from the website back-end,
  then shows it on the attacker's own site.
  (In this demo the back-end's account data is the Pocket ID user profile it fronts.)
  Where Tide closes it:
    Going in   - subresource integrity: the swapped-in script never loads, so none of this runs.
    Coming out - DPoP / sender-constrained session: bound to the device, useless on another site.
end note

@enduml
